<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>dev.nkap</groupId>
    <artifactId>nkap-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>
  <artifactId>nkap-test-support</artifactId>
  <name>Nkap :: Test support</name>

  <!--
    Test infrastructure more than one module needs, kept once (issue #214): booting a
    simulator application and driving its control plane (SimulatorUnderTest, with
    SimulatorStartupLog), and checking a hand-written record toString() (RecordToString).
    Before this module each consumer kept its own copy, because a class in one module's test
    sources cannot be depended on by another.

    Test infrastructure only. Its classes sit in src/main so other modules can use them, but
    nothing may depend on this module at any scope but test.

    Why it is not nkap-conformance: that kit depends on provider-api and JUnit, nothing else,
    and every contributor inherits whatever it gains. SimulatorUnderTest needs Spring Boot and
    Tomcat, and RecordToString AssertJ. Nothing here is deployed to a Maven repository, so the
    cost is not to outside consumers; it is a discipline this repository holds itself to
    (conformance/pom.xml and its README keep the kit off any simulator; CONTRIBUTING.md
    points every contributor at it). What costs the kit nothing, CallbackReceiver, is in the
    kit.

    What may not go in it: a dependency on any nkap module. Without one, any module can use
    it at test scope without a cycle, simulator-mpesa and server included. A helper that needs
    a particular simulator takes that simulator's application class as a parameter, as
    SimulatorUnderTest does.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
      <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
